within Pharmacolibrary.Drugs.B_BloodAndBloodFormingOrgans.B01A_AntithromboticAgents.B01AF01_Rivaroxaban;
model Rivaroxaban_Esmaeili2022_PD_pt
  "linear exposure-response sweep — Esmaeili_2022 :: PT (prothrombin time)"
  extends Pharmacolibrary.Interfaces.PartialExposureResponseSweep(
    E0 = 12.6,      // [s; seconds × 1]
    Emax = 0, EC50 = 1,      // unused by a line
    exposure_per_s = 1e-06);
  parameter Real slope = 18000   "[meter ** 3 * second / kilogram; s·ml·ng-1 × 1e+06]";
equation
  response = E0 + slope*exposure;
  annotation(experiment(StartTime = 0, StopTime = 1000, Interval = 2),
    Documentation(info = "<html><body><h4>Rivaroxaban_Esmaeili2022_PD_pt</h4><p>The steady-state exposure-response curve of Esmaeili T et al., <i>Journal of clinical pharmacy and therapeutics 47(8):1284–1292</i> 2022 (<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/jcpt.13673\">doi:10.1111/jcpt.13673</a>) — prothrombin time [seconds] against rivaroxaban ng·ml-1 — obtained by sweeping the driver instead of simulating a dose. Model family: linear.</p><h4>The parameters, in the paper's terms</h4><pre>  E0    =       12.6 seconds  drug-free response (baseline) (baseline, Q324)
  slope =      0.018 s·ml·ng-1 change in response per unit of exposure (slope, Q335)</pre><p>The algebra is <code>response = E0 + slope*exposure</code>; this model only supplies the numbers. The x axis is one ng·ml-1 of rivaroxaban per second of simulated time (exposure is in kg/m3): there is no state, no dose and no kinetics — plot <code>response</code> against <code>exposure</code> (or <code>time</code>).</p><h4>What it reproduces</h4><pre>  exposure = 0         response 12.6   (drug-free)</pre></body></html>", revisions = "<html><body><ul><li>11/2026 generated by pharmacolibrary-dev export.pharmacolibrary_models from Esmaeili_2022: parameters extracted from the paper by the pharmacolibrary-dev pipeline, model built by its engineer, reviewed: curated candidate</li></ul></body></html>"));
end Rivaroxaban_Esmaeili2022_PD_pt;
